A||N||3||Operative1010||Where is the oil pressure sender on an '88 2.5L 4-Cyl?||||1||
Z||000000||Operative1010||11-10-2001||01:24 PM||||I get very low (orange zone) readings on my oil pressure guage after the vehicle reaches normal operating temperature. Sometimes its jumpy and erratic for short periods. There is no reason to believe that the actual oil pressure is low.<P>I suspect my oil pressure sender or the wires running to it are the cause, but I do not know where this gadget is located on my car to check it. <P>My greatest concern with this pressure reading at or near zero would be if my fuel pump may cut out since supposedly the fuel pump is driven by the sender after the oil pressure goes above 4psi (the first two seconds of the engine starting up.) So far this hasn't been a problem.<P>Can anyone tell me what I am looking for?<BR>Thanks!<BR>||||reg||1
Z||000001||theogre||11-10-2001||01:52 PM||||Unless they moved it in 88, it is under the intake manifold screwed into the side of the block and is a turd to get to.<P>------------------<BR>  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/fullstaff.gif">  <FONT COLOR="blue"><I><B>11-Sept-01, The day the world as we knew it ended.</B></I></FONT>||||reg||1
Z||000002||Operative1010||11-12-2001||10:54 AM||||btt  <P>Thanks ogre...but is there any chance anyone else can be even more specific...im really pretty clueless on this and Im wondering how much time to allow to get at it....also is there a concern it could be difficult to remove?||||reg||1
Z||000003||FieroJoe||11-12-2001||12:56 PM||||Get under your car, get on your back with your head facing the front of the car.  Slide under the car, just until your head is to the front side of your muffler (make sure it's cool).  Above you, you will see your DIS ignition coil pack and all the spark plug wires running upwards, and if you look straight up you will be looking at the underside of your intake manifold.  Screwed into the block, is the oil pressure sender, right beneath the two intake runners for the two middle cylinders.  The oil pressure sender has a 3 wire connector on it, and is large and round.  You have to press on the sides of the connector in order for it to release.  Check the terminals on both the sensor and inside the connector.  Clean out any corrosion and use a little dielectric grease to prevent further corrosion when you put the connector back together.||||reg||1
